WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:02.744
- [Announcer] This week on Sound Field.

00:00:02.744 --> 00:00:05.860
Since the beginning of
time a series of numbers

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:08.044
has inspired the world around us.

00:00:08.044 --> 00:00:11.660
This number can be seen
almost anywhere you look.

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:13.840
It's called the golden ratio

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:15.750
and you might have seen it before

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:18.458
but did you know that
you can also hear it?

00:00:18.458 --> 00:00:21.490
(buzzing low tones)

00:00:21.490 --> 00:00:23.300
- Have you ever noticed
how some pieces of music

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:25.370
just seem to make sense?

00:00:25.370 --> 00:00:29.630
The notes, the chords, phrases,
the dynamics and harmony,

00:00:29.630 --> 00:00:32.270
they can all feel like they
were meant to go together.

00:00:32.270 --> 00:00:34.280
Well, many people believe
that this isn't simply

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:38.150
a coincidence but part of a
natural order to the universe,

00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:40.590
something called the golden ratio.

00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:43.090
To explain the golden ratio
we asked our friend Joe

00:00:43.090 --> 00:00:45.750
from It's Okay To Be Smart to fill us in.

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:49.320
- The golden ratio is
the irrational number Phi

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:51.960
and like Pi, it doesn't end.

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:54.867
So instead of saying 1.6180339887

00:00:57.260 --> 00:00:59.910
and so on, we'll just say Phi.

00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:02.230
So what's interesting about Phi?

00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:04.223
Take this golden rectangle.

00:01:04.223 --> 00:01:09.223
It's golden because the ratio
between its sides match Phi.

00:01:09.310 --> 00:01:11.280
If you cut a square
off a golden rectangle,

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:13.520
you create a smaller rectangle

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:15.780
with the same golden proportions.

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:19.070
And because this long irrational
number made sense visually

00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:21.490
but couldn't be explained as a fraction,

00:01:21.490 --> 00:01:24.450
some ancient philosophers
figured it must have

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:25.670
a higher meaning.

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:27.570
They called it the golden ratio and later

00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:30.022
the divine proportion.

00:01:30.022 --> 00:01:31.990
(dramatic voices)

00:01:31.990 --> 00:01:35.162
Which brings us to something
called the Fibonacci Sequence.

00:01:35.162 --> 00:01:35.995
This pattern starts with zero,

00:01:35.995 --> 00:01:40.583
then each following number is
the sum of the two before it.

00:01:41.530 --> 00:01:44.530
And what does that have to
do with the golden ratio?

00:01:44.530 --> 00:01:46.340
Well as the Sequence goes higher,

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:48.160
the ratio between the numbers

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:52.070
gets closer and closer to 1.618

00:01:52.070 --> 00:01:52.980
or Phi.

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:57.133
Many believe the Sequence
could explain growth in nature.

00:01:57.133 --> 00:02:00.186
If you connect each corner
of the squares with an arc

00:02:00.186 --> 00:02:02.393
you'll get a golden spiral.

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:04.933
Look familiar?

00:02:04.933 --> 00:02:08.110
Well, a lot of people see these
golden spirals everywhere.

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:11.027
(futuristic music)

00:02:12.082 --> 00:02:13.930
- I didn't know anything
about golden ratio.

00:02:13.930 --> 00:02:16.496
I was reading, trying to
learn like, okay (laughs).

00:02:16.496 --> 00:02:18.550
- In school I learned it as,

00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:20.870
okay, a piece of music
has a golden section

00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:23.053
which is that point, that climactic,

00:02:23.053 --> 00:02:25.780
it doesn't always have to be
dramatic but just something

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:27.220
special always happen.

00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:29.440
Music theorists have claimed
to find the golden ratio

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:32.230
in the works of many
famous classical composers

00:02:32.230 --> 00:02:34.210
from Mozart to Debussy.

00:02:34.210 --> 00:02:36.970
Some say the golden ratio
and the Fibonacci Sequence

00:02:36.970 --> 00:02:40.090
are evident in Music for
Strings, Percussion and Celesta

00:02:40.090 --> 00:02:42.430
by Hungarian composer, Bela Bartok.

00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:44.720
For example, the opening xylophone solo

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:46.980
in the third movement
has a rhythmic pattern

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:48.930
following the Fibonacci Sequence

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:53.390
going from 1, 2, 3, 5,
8 and then back down

00:02:53.390 --> 00:02:55.447
5, 3, 2, 1.

00:02:55.447 --> 00:02:58.614
(chiming bell sounds)

00:03:05.270 --> 00:03:07.420
- So Bartok I think was
being accused of being

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:09.263
really cerebral in his music,

00:03:09.263 --> 00:03:13.240
so he was pretty notoriously
silent about his work.

00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:15.550
You don't see notes in the margin

00:03:15.550 --> 00:03:17.154
with these little details, right?

00:03:17.154 --> 00:03:18.750
So a lot of people have said, well look,

00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:19.610
if he was doing this,

00:03:19.610 --> 00:03:21.210
he was using Fibonacci
and the golden ratio,

00:03:21.210 --> 00:03:23.050
you know, why didn't he tell people

00:03:23.050 --> 00:03:24.610
or why wasn't it more obvious?

00:03:24.610 --> 00:03:27.140
- Couldn't it just be that certain pieces

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:30.900
that have that lineup with
the golden ratio stand out

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:33.560
and then music theorists
gravitate towards that

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:34.890
as an example?

00:03:34.890 --> 00:03:36.230
- Yes, absolutely.

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:38.290
For example, the Celesta.

00:03:38.290 --> 00:03:41.061
That comes in at bar 77
in the first movement

00:03:41.061 --> 00:03:43.870
and that has nothing
to do with golden ratio

00:03:43.870 --> 00:03:44.837
or Fibonacci.

00:03:44.837 --> 00:03:45.670
(beep)

00:03:45.670 --> 00:03:48.070
But the piece in the
title is Celesta, right?

00:03:48.070 --> 00:03:49.800
So that's really important, right?

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:51.930
So does that mean we
discount all the other stuff?

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:52.763
I mean, I think you're right.

00:03:52.763 --> 00:03:54.640
You have to look at the big picture.

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:57.450
- But in your opinion
why should people care

00:03:57.450 --> 00:03:58.660
about the golden ratio?

00:03:58.660 --> 00:04:01.070
- Well (laughs), so what
we haven't talked about

00:04:01.070 --> 00:04:02.940
is how it shows up in nature.

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:05.210
So, for example, if you
go out and look at flowers

00:04:05.210 --> 00:04:06.662
and you start counting flower petals,

00:04:06.662 --> 00:04:09.634
most of the time you'll
find a Fibonacci number.

00:04:09.634 --> 00:04:11.400
Think about, like, if you
were gonna grow and plant

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:13.915
lots of seeds on a flower
you wouldn't just wanna

00:04:13.915 --> 00:04:15.840
equally spread them out.

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:17.570
As you got further away from the center

00:04:17.570 --> 00:04:18.468
there'd be too much space.

00:04:18.468 --> 00:04:20.310
Nature doesn't want that, right?

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:22.060
Nature wants the sunflower to procreate

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:23.680
so the more seeds the better.

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:25.271
It turns out the optimal angle

00:04:25.271 --> 00:04:27.031
of where they're arranging themselves

00:04:27.031 --> 00:04:29.320
is related to the golden ratio.

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:31.730
So put that all together and
you've got kind of a nice,

00:04:31.730 --> 00:04:33.086
beautiful, like, mother nature.

00:04:33.086 --> 00:04:36.740
What could be more beautiful
than mountains and flowers

00:04:36.740 --> 00:04:37.860
and streams, right?

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:39.120
Perhaps that's why musicians

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:40.655
have gravitated towards it in terms of,

00:04:40.655 --> 00:04:42.930
you're writing a piece of
music and you have a climax

00:04:42.930 --> 00:04:43.763
in your piece,

00:04:43.763 --> 00:04:45.207
where're you gonna put it?

00:04:45.207 --> 00:04:46.430
Are you gonna put it right in the middle?

00:04:46.430 --> 00:04:48.070
No, you're gonna put
it a little off-center.

00:04:48.070 --> 00:04:50.730
So maybe you tend to
gravitate towards something

00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:51.880
like the golden ratio.

00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:54.040
- The climax of Barok's
first movement happens

00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:56.722
at bar 55 which is not
only a Fibonacci number

00:04:56.722 --> 00:04:59.893
but also lands very close
to the golden ratio.

00:04:59.893 --> 00:05:03.920
("Music for Strings, Percussion
and Celesta" by Bartok)

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:05.310
Music theorists call this

00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:06.770
a Phi moment.

00:05:06.770 --> 00:05:09.330
- Bartok isn't the only
composer to have a Phi moment.

00:05:09.330 --> 00:05:10.360
Finding one is simple.

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:12.500
Take the length of a
song and then multiply it

00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:15.080
by 0.618 the inverse of Phi.

00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:15.913
For example,

00:05:15.913 --> 00:05:17.990
take Under Pressure by
Queen and David Bowie.

00:05:17.990 --> 00:05:19.040
We have a total

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:22.260
of 246 seconds times 0.618

00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:24.250
which equals 152 seconds.

00:05:24.250 --> 00:05:26.650
Now listen to what happens
at that exact moment.

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:33.310
If you look hard enough, you'll
start finding it everywhere.

00:05:33.310 --> 00:05:34.960
Check out Drake's In My Feelings.

00:05:40.460 --> 00:05:42.750
But were all these musicians
writin' songs with calculators

00:05:42.750 --> 00:05:44.070
by their side?

00:05:44.070 --> 00:05:45.230
I doubt it.

00:05:45.230 --> 00:05:47.570
Some say we find these
golden moments because humans

00:05:47.570 --> 00:05:50.200
are just hard-wired to
find order in the world.

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:52.470
- But sometimes the order
of the Fibonacci Sequence

00:05:52.470 --> 00:05:55.310
can be mesmerizing like
in this konnakol rhythm

00:05:55.310 --> 00:05:56.725
by B.C. Manjunath.

00:05:56.725 --> 00:05:58.174
(clapping and vocalizing)

00:05:58.174 --> 00:06:01.020
Manjunath wrote this as a
tribute piece after the death

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:03.460
of his father who had introduced
the Fibonacci Sequence

00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:04.980
to him as a teenager.

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:08.260
This style of music is carnatic
music from southern India

00:06:08.260 --> 00:06:11.450
and is characterized by a
very complex rhythmic system.

00:06:11.450 --> 00:06:13.640
Manjunath used the first eight numbers

00:06:13.640 --> 00:06:16.352
of the Fibonacci Sequence to
compose the intricate rhythms

00:06:16.352 --> 00:06:17.930
in this piece.

00:06:17.930 --> 00:06:21.111
By the way, this is the
Phi moment of this video.

00:06:21.111 --> 00:06:23.944
(trumpet fanfare)

00:06:24.980 --> 00:06:29.230
(rapid rhythmic vocalizing)

00:06:29.230 --> 00:06:33.490
- I did write a piece
using certain elements

00:06:33.490 --> 00:06:35.900
of the golden proportions

00:06:35.900 --> 00:06:38.140
and the Fibonacci numbers.

00:06:38.140 --> 00:06:39.480
Should I play it for you?

00:06:39.480 --> 00:06:41.363
It's really fresh, but okay.

00:06:41.363 --> 00:06:42.196
- Oh, you're gonna play it live.

00:06:42.196 --> 00:06:43.360
Oh, let's get it.

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:44.383
I wanna hear this, yes.

00:06:44.383 --> 00:06:46.550
(rhythmic gentle piano)

00:06:46.550 --> 00:06:51.200
- So what I did was I just
came up with a little motif

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:55.070
and then I fleshed it out
until I felt like it could

00:06:55.070 --> 00:06:57.360
go somewhere and something can change.

00:06:57.360 --> 00:07:00.030
Then I knew, okay, this
is the golden section

00:07:00.030 --> 00:07:04.010
and depending on the rules
of the golden proportions

00:07:04.010 --> 00:07:08.201
I know exactly how much time
is required until the end.

00:07:08.201 --> 00:07:10.342
(rhythmic gentle piano)

00:07:10.342 --> 00:07:13.092
(fast arpeggios)

00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:15.185
Okay, I'll stop

00:07:15.185 --> 00:07:16.283
- Woo hoo hoo!
right there (laughs).

00:07:16.283 --> 00:07:18.266
So obviously here,

00:07:18.266 --> 00:07:20.170
(fast arpeggios)

00:07:20.170 --> 00:07:21.153
it changes, right?

00:07:21.153 --> 00:07:21.986
- Yeah.

00:07:21.986 --> 00:07:23.950
- So I try to make it
as obvious as possible.

00:07:23.950 --> 00:07:26.500
But that is at the point

00:07:26.500 --> 00:07:29.210
where 200 sixteenth notes

00:07:29.210 --> 00:07:32.320
have passed and the whole piece

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:36.650
has 324 sixteenth notes.

00:07:36.650 --> 00:07:38.690
So that point at 200

00:07:38.690 --> 00:07:39.800
- Where it takes from there

00:07:39.800 --> 00:07:42.546
is the golden section
- Da da da da da da da.

00:07:42.546 --> 00:07:46.273
or the Phi moment and this pattern,

00:07:46.273 --> 00:07:49.023
(fast arpeggios)

00:07:50.650 --> 00:07:52.120
I used the Fibonacci numbers

00:07:52.120 --> 00:07:54.811
so this has five sixteenth notes

00:07:54.811 --> 00:07:56.020
(slow arpeggios)

00:07:56.020 --> 00:07:58.377
then it goes to eight sixteenth notes

00:07:58.377 --> 00:08:00.345
(medium arpeggios)

00:08:00.345 --> 00:08:03.139
and then it goes to 13
sixteenth notes (laughs)

00:08:03.139 --> 00:08:05.760
(fast arpeggios)

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:07.260
and then it goes back down.

00:08:07.260 --> 00:08:08.620
- Ooo!

00:08:08.620 --> 00:08:09.800
- I don't know.

00:08:09.800 --> 00:08:13.410
So what was hard was it is has to end

00:08:13.410 --> 00:08:16.760
by, what, 324 sixteenth notes, right?

00:08:16.760 --> 00:08:18.437
So it's like how I'm gonna end this

00:08:18.437 --> 00:08:19.810
(fast arpeggios)

00:08:19.810 --> 00:08:21.687
so I just bring back the pedal.

00:08:21.687 --> 00:08:24.437
(fast arpeggios)

00:08:26.145 --> 00:08:29.145
(soft piano chords)

00:08:31.370 --> 00:08:33.980
I just kind of try to evaporate it

00:08:33.980 --> 00:08:36.110
but that was difficult
- Not right, not right,

00:08:36.110 --> 00:08:36.943
about this
- Not right.

00:08:36.943 --> 00:08:37.842
because I was working
- Not right.

00:08:37.842 --> 00:08:39.134
with the structure.
- Not right.

00:08:39.134 --> 00:08:40.564
But (laughs),

00:08:40.564 --> 00:08:42.303
- You are really the truth.

00:08:42.303 --> 00:08:43.136
(laughs)

00:08:43.136 --> 00:08:44.761
You're really good at what you do.

00:08:44.761 --> 00:08:46.190
- oh, thank you.
That was really tight.

00:08:46.190 --> 00:08:50.410
- Using this type of format,
this type of structure,

00:08:50.410 --> 00:08:54.840
is another way of adding a
limitation to what you're doing,

00:08:54.840 --> 00:08:58.450
some sort of puzzle to work around

00:08:58.450 --> 00:09:00.740
so I found it really stimulating.

00:09:00.740 --> 00:09:02.169
- People think of
limitations and boundaries

00:09:02.169 --> 00:09:06.590
as negative words, however,
when you are creative

00:09:06.590 --> 00:09:08.080
and have so many ideas coming to you,

00:09:08.080 --> 00:09:11.355
you kind of need those
walls to create within.

00:09:11.355 --> 00:09:13.560
- Exactly and it's so true.

00:09:13.560 --> 00:09:16.972
Just the template aspect of
it is very, very valuable

00:09:16.972 --> 00:09:18.534
as a composer.

00:09:18.534 --> 00:09:19.720
Since you've heard it,

00:09:19.720 --> 00:09:23.280
can you help me turn it into a piece

00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:26.240
with perhaps some percussion
- Yes.

00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:27.469
or some drum sounds?

00:09:27.469 --> 00:09:28.310
- Yes.

00:09:28.310 --> 00:09:30.050
- Maybe something with clapping.

00:09:30.050 --> 00:09:32.440
- Yeah, I got some sounds for that.

00:09:32.440 --> 00:09:33.430
I got some stuff for that.

00:09:33.430 --> 00:09:34.263
- Okay.

00:09:34.263 --> 00:09:35.709
- I wanna, I'm excited to
dance to that first section,

00:09:35.709 --> 00:09:38.104
to da da da da da da, da da da da da da.

00:09:38.104 --> 00:09:41.688
(rhythmic vocalizations)

00:09:41.688 --> 00:09:43.396
(laughs)

00:09:43.396 --> 00:09:44.229
I was
- Cool.

00:09:44.229 --> 00:09:45.087
diggin' the build up.

00:09:45.087 --> 00:09:45.920
- Cool.
I was diggin' that too.

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:49.003
(upbeat piano music)

00:09:55.403 --> 00:09:59.236
(fast arpeggios and clapping)

00:10:01.301 --> 00:10:03.243
Where do you notice the golden ratio?

00:10:03.243 --> 00:10:05.573
What do you think of our
mathematical composition?

00:10:05.573 --> 00:10:08.557
Comment below and don't
forget to subscribe.

00:10:16.155 --> 00:10:18.808
(futuristic tones)

